# OPOS — Company OS Framework

OPOS is an AI-native company operating system expressed as a GitHub repo of markdown and JSON. It encodes your company (mission, policies, knowledge), your departments (charters, data, processes), your roles (agents), and your work-in-flight (backlogs) using Claude Code's native cascading primitives. No runtime, no orchestration code — just templates and folder conventions you fork and adapt.

OPOS is distributed as a **Copier template**: scaffold a new company instance with one command, then receive upstream updates via the agent-driven `sync-from-core` skill or the opt-in GitHub Actions workflow.

## Scaffold a new company instance

**Prerequisites:**
- Python 3.10+
- `copier` ≥9.0.0 — install via `pip install copier` (or `pipx install copier`)
- `git`
- `gh` CLI authenticated against your GitHub account (`gh auth login`)
- A GitHub account with read access to `Koroqe/OPOS`

**Scaffold:**
```bash
copier copy --vcs-ref v0.5.0 gh:Koroqe/OPOS my-company-os -d COMPANY_NAME="My Company"
cd my-company-os
git init && git add -A && git commit -m "chore: initial OPOS scaffold (v0.5.0)"
gh repo create my-company-os --private --source=. --push
```

That gives you a fresh OPOS instance with all CORE files (agents, skills, templates) pre-installed and {% raw %}`{{ COMPANY_NAME }}`{% endraw %} substituted to your company name. The STARTER files (example departments, configs) ship once for you to customize — `copier update` will never overwrite them. **As of v0.5.0**, root `CLAUDE.md` is also consumer-owned post-scaffold (so your edits to Mission and Values survive future `copier update` runs).

## First steps after scaffold

After `copier copy` finishes, your repo is structurally complete but content-blank in six places: Mission, Values, strategic priorities, dept missions (the 6 v0.5.1 default depts: rnd, finance, people, legal, commercial, pr), and the policies folder. Run the `company-setup` skill (owned by `coo`) to fill these in conversationally:

1. Open the repo in your editor (VS Code recommended for the inline Claude Code session).
2. Open a Claude Code session at the repo root.
3. Invoke `/company-setup`. ~10 questions; ~15 minutes total. Progress is written to files as you go.

When complete, browse your populated OS via `/serve-console` (opens `http://127.0.0.1:8765/`). The skill will print the exact next-step commands at the end.

If you re-run `/company-setup` on a populated repo it will refuse — to re-do, restore the `<one short sentence>` placeholder in root `CLAUDE.md`'s Mission section first.

## Updating from upstream

Two ways to receive updates from `Koroqe/OPOS`:

1. **Agent-driven (DEFAULT — no infrastructure).** `check-for-updates` is invoked automatically as step 1 of `task-register`, `task-update`, `task-complete`. When the upstream has a newer release, you see a one-line notice in the skill output. Run `/sync-from-core` when you're ready to apply.
2. **Opt-in GitHub Actions (background PRs).** Edit `.github/workflows/sync-opos.yml` and uncomment the `schedule:` block to enable weekly auto-PRs (Mon 3am UTC). Runs on GitHub's servers; your laptop is uninvolved.

See [`RISKS.md`](RISKS.md) Risks 9–13 for known limitations (manual conflict resolution, rollback, breaking-change migration).

## Quickstart (legacy — no longer needed; Copier handles substitution)

1. Clone this repo (or fork it). _Note: prefer `copier copy` above; this manual flow is for framework developers._
2. Substitute the framework tokens for your company. List every remaining token with:
   ```bash
   grep -rn "<<[A-Z_]*>>" . --include="*.md"
   ```
   The primary token is {% raw %}`{{ COMPANY_NAME }}`{% endraw %}. Once substituted, this command should return zero matches.
3. Pick one department to start with. The `departments/rnd/` folder is a worked example with both research and engineering content (engineering folded in as the building branch at v0.5.1) — use it as the reference for adapting another dept's structure.
4. Add real MCP servers in `.mcp.json` and configure per-agent access via each agent's `tools:` allow-list.
5. Open Claude Code at the repo root and verify the smoke test (see [`RISKS.md`](RISKS.md) for the recipe).

## The cascade model

Claude Code automatically walks the directory tree from your session's working directory upward, loading every `CLAUDE.md` it finds. A session opened in `departments/rnd/` sees:

```
departments/rnd/
        ↑ (closer files load last, highest priority)
departments/rnd/CLAUDE.md           ← dept charter (R&D umbrella; eng-lead + eng-reviewer report up)
        ↑
company/CLAUDE.md                   ← if cascade passes through it
        ↑
CLAUDE.md                           ← root constitution
```

Skills (`.claude/skills/<name>/`) nest similarly. Subagents (`.claude/agents/**/*.md`) are scanned recursively from the repo root.

## Where things live

| Primitive | Nests by folder? | Location | Notes |
|-----------|------------------|----------|-------|
| `CLAUDE.md` | YES — auto-cascades up the directory tree | any folder | Use for constitution + dept charters |
| Skills (`.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`) | YES — nested `.claude/skills/` work | repo root or any dept folder | Each dept can host its own skills |
| Subagents (`.claude/agents/**/*.md`) | YES — recursive scan; subdir is purely organizational; identity = `name` frontmatter | `.claude/agents/<dept>/<role>.md` | Subfolders for visual grouping |
| Slash commands | Merged into skills per docs; prefer skills | n/a | `.claude/commands/X.md` and `.claude/skills/X/SKILL.md` are equivalent |
| Settings (`.claude/settings.json`) | NO — project root only | `.claude/settings.json` | Per-dept settings are not supported |
| MCP (`.mcp.json`) | Root-only | repo root | Per-agent access via `tools:` allow-list |

## Templates

Every artifact in the repo is a copy-with-substitution of one of these:

- [`shared/templates/CLAUDE.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/CLAUDE.md.tmpl) — scope-level constitution / charter
- [`shared/templates/AGENT.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/AGENT.md.tmpl) — role definition with delegation pattern
- [`shared/templates/SKILL.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/SKILL.md.tmpl) — runnable form of a process
- [`shared/templates/PROCESS.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/PROCESS.md.tmpl) — process-as-data (owner, inputs, success criteria)
- [`shared/templates/BACKLOG-ITEM.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/BACKLOG-ITEM.md.tmpl) — one-off task with runs log
- [`shared/templates/task-issue.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/task-issue.md.tmpl) — GitHub issue body for tracked tasks
- [`shared/templates/task-update.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/task-update.md.tmpl) — comment body for mid-task progress updates
- [`shared/templates/TASK.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/TASK.md.tmpl) — per-task markdown source-of-truth file written to `tasks/<issue-number>.md` by `task-register` (NEW in v0.2.0)

Each template's header lists its substitution tokens. The convention is **copy-then-substitute**, not symlink or runtime reference — templates are scaffolding, the copies are the artifacts.

## The design loop

The framework's process-creation primitive is itself a skill: [`design-process`](.claude/skills/design-process/), owned by the [`ops-manager`](.claude/agents/company/ops-manager.md) agent.

1. A new repeatable job arrives — either as a fresh job description from the user, or as a backlog item (`BACKLOG-ITEM.md`) the user is ready to formalize.
2. `ops-manager` reads the framework (root `CLAUDE.md`, templates, an existing skill as reference), identifies which departments are involved by enumerating `departments/*/CLAUDE.md`, and consults each involved dept lead via the `Task` subagent tool.
3. `ops-manager` drafts a `SKILL.md` + `PROCESS.md` pair and presents the proposal in chat along with a summary of consultations and open questions.
4. The user iterates with `ops-manager` until satisfied, then approves explicitly ("write it," "ship it," etc.).
5. On approval, the files are written to the chosen path (global or dept-scoped), the owner agent's advisory `owns_processes:` is updated, and (if a backlog item was the input) it's flipped to `state: designed` with a `designed_as:` pointer.

Backlog items remain a useful notebook for ideas and one-offs that aren't yet formalized — see [`departments/rnd/backlog/example-add-rollback-step.md`](departments/rnd/backlog/example-add-rollback-step.md) for a worked example of an engineering-flavored item in `state: active` that hasn't yet been fed into `design-process` (moved from `departments/engineering/` to `departments/rnd/` at v0.5.1 when engineering folded into the R&D umbrella).

## The task-tracking loop

Three sibling skills track each new task as a GitHub issue, owned by [`chief-of-staff`](.claude/agents/company/chief-of-staff.md):

- [`task-register`](.claude/skills/task-register/) — open a GitHub issue at the start of a new task; capture title, departments, plan link, goal. Records the active issue number in `.claude/.current-task` AND creates `tasks/<issue-number>.md` from `TASK.md.tmpl`.
- [`task-update`](.claude/skills/task-update/) — mid-execution, post a progress comment and optionally patch the issue's `**Status:**` line. Idempotent via a per-update `--key`.
- [`task-complete`](.claude/skills/task-complete/) — at completion, post a final report (agent summary + auto-generated `git log` changelog + PR links + deliverables), apply the `status:done` label, close the issue, and archive `tasks/<n>.md` to `tasks/closed/`.
- [`task-pause`](.claude/skills/task-pause/) (NEW in v0.2.0) — pause the active task; moves the issue number from `.current-task` to `.paused-tasks`. The GitHub issue stays open with status `blocked`.
- [`task-resume`](.claude/skills/task-resume/) (NEW in v0.2.0) — resume a paused task; moves the issue number from `.paused-tasks` back to `.current-task`. Status flips to `in_progress`.

The target GitHub repo is configured in [`.claude/task-tracking.config.json`](.claude/task-tracking.config.json). All five skills shell out to the `gh` CLI via Bash; the `gh` CLI must be authenticated on the machine. Department labels follow the `dept:<name>` convention (lowercased; auto-created if missing — color from `_label_palette` in the config).

## The AI-first kernel (NEW in v0.5.1)

OPOS now ships a specific organizational philosophy as starter content. When ANY dept surfaces a capability gap — "we need to be able to do X" — `people-lead` runs [`allocate-resource`](.claude/skills/allocate-resource/) which executes the **4-question AI-first decision tree**:

1. Is the core work text-based (reading documents, writing content, analyzing data)?
2. Does the work AVOID requiring physical-world action (driving, on-site presence, hands-on physical work)?
3. Does the work AVOID requiring legally-mandated human accountability (signing as a licensed professional, sworn testimony)?
4. Does the work AVOID requiring lived human experience as a primary input (judging mood in a room, building trust face-to-face)?

**All 4 yes → AI route**: `allocate-resource` emits a `/design-agent` recommendation; the founder runs it; [`ops-manager`](.claude/agents/company/ops-manager.md) owns `design-agent` and creates the new AI agent.

**Any no → human route**: `allocate-resource` writes a job spec to `company/hiring/<slug>.md` using [`shared/templates/HIRING-SPEC.md.tmpl`](shared/templates/HIRING-SPEC.md.tmpl). The `why_not_ai:` frontmatter field records WHICH question failed (audit trail). `ceo` reviews + flips the state to `approved`.

The 6 default departments are designed around this philosophy: every dept lead (`finance-lead`, `legal-lead`, `commercial-lead`, `pr-lead`, `rnd-lead`, `eng-lead`) starts with tools sized for AI-first execution (LLM-strength work like content generation, document analysis, contract review) and consults `allocate-resource` before any human-hire conversation.

## Console (read-only browser)

Run `python3 ui/console.py` (or invoke the [`serve-console`](.claude/skills/serve-console/) skill) to start a local-host read-only web UI over the framework files. Defaults to `http://127.0.0.1:8765/`. Five pages plus four detail variants:

- **Dashboard** — counts (tasks active/paused/completed, agents, skills, departments) + the 5 most recent activity entries.
- **Tasks** — CRM-style table with state / dept / owner filters; per-task detail renders `tasks/<n>.md` plus the GitHub issue body and comments (fetched via `gh issue view`; 30s in-process cache; degrades gracefully if `gh` is unauthenticated).
- **Agents** — grouped by department; per-agent detail shows frontmatter, markdown body, owned-skill backlinks, and a "called by" reverse lookup.
- **Skills** — grouped by owner agent; per-skill detail renders SKILL.md + PROCESS.md + a "Recent runs" link list pointing into the activity feed.
- **Departments** — list + per-dept detail (charter, member agents, dept-scoped skills).
- **Activity** — chronological history-entry feed across all skills with skill / outcome / since filters.

Prereqs: Python 3.10+, `jinja2` (already a Copier transitive dep), `markdown` (`pip install markdown` or `pipx inject copier markdown`). See [`ui/README.md`](ui/README.md) for full install + run details and [`RISKS.md`](RISKS.md) Risk 16 for the localhost-binding caveat.

## Self-improvement log schema

Every process records each run in its own `history/` folder. Files are named `YYYY-MM-DD-<run-id>.md` with frontmatter:

- `date`: YYYY-MM-DD
- `run_id`: short id unique within the skill
- `skill`: skill name
- `actor`: agent name or "human"
- `outcome`: `success` | `partial` | `failure`
- `duration_min`: integer
- `proposed_delta`: free text describing a proposed SKILL.md/PROCESS.md change, or "none"
- `status`: `open` | `applied` | `rejected`

Owner agents review their history folders periodically and convert `open` deltas into updates to their own PROCESS.md (which itself can be promoted into a `history/` entry of its own — the loop closes).

## Conventions

- **Agent → process binding** lives in `PROCESS.md` `owner:` (single source of truth). Agent `owns_processes:` is advisory — keep it in sync but trust the PROCESS.md.
- **JSON config files** carry no inline comments. `.mcp.json` and `.claude/settings.json` are strict JSON; commentary about them lives in root `CLAUDE.md` and this README.
- **Restricted folders** (e.g. `company/strategy/`) are honored by convention only. See [`RISKS.md`](RISKS.md) for hardening paths.

## Known limitations

- Restricted-data enforcement is convention-only — see [`RISKS.md`](RISKS.md) Risk 1.
- Per-department `.claude/settings.json` is not supported by Claude Code — see [`RISKS.md`](RISKS.md) Risk 3.

